ay that i'm wrong um and  you can use all sorts of tolerances you  can use  a distance um and play around with those  as well all right so um one more option  um or example of the many options of  assessment function  are you can use patterns or entities  so right you don't want to have to write  out all of the different options  you can just use the built-in patterns  and entities that  are already in wolfram language so  you can ask a student to provide an even  number for example  if we run this we give them the answer  too  we love it but if we say three  or it's angry um and  similarly we can do it for something  like name a european country  using the entities and then if we give  it germany  it's it works so  to build off of that a little bit i just  wanted to show you  the partial credit and element-wise  assessment  so instead of just listing one country  um you can have them list  multiple countries so and grade each of  those  independently so let's run this  and if we run our example here it gives  us this  which we know are right but i think it's  more interesting if we actually look at  what's inside of this  let's just look at everything um  right you can see that our score is two  where  it's default is one point per correct  answer  um and we got two um overall we were  correct  this was what the student provided there  was no explanations from the teacher  but we also get a breakdown of all of  the different  scores and whether they were correct per  answer  so if we put in one that is incorrect  you can see that our last option was  false which will give us  an overall false because we have to be  correct for the whole thing by default  um but for fun  let's throw in another option here so  if we type in answer  correct  a very short option and then  we can say that if any of them are true  we will accept them  okay um so if we run that  it should give us this answer correct  should turn from false  to true like magic  okay so right you can custom